This document describes formats called Basic Streaming Encoding Rules
(BSER) and Distinguished Streaming Encoding Rules (DSER), which are
variants of BER and DER (but are not compatible with them; a different
decoder must be used to decode them). These formats may be useful when
the program producing the data needs to do so without knowing how long
it is before it is finished (like the indefinite length encoding in BER
is, although I think this way is a bit simpler and cleaner).

The ASN.1 and ASN.1X types work like they do in BER and DER except that
strings must be primitive rather than constructed.

An item in BSER starts with the type, which is encoded the same as BER.

If it is constructed, then the length is omitted but then the items inside
follow and then a single byte 0x00 terminates the construction.

If it is primitive, then it consists of zero or more segments and then a
single byte 0x00 to terminate the value. Each segment has a length from 1
to 255 bytes, and consists of one byte specifying the length and then the
data of that length. The value is the concatenation of all segments.

DSER is BSER with the additional restrictions of DER, and another
additional restriction that a segment of a primitive value that is
not the last segment must have length 255.

My opinion is that CER is not very good. DSER is more compact than CER as
well as simpler than CER, and does not need construced strings.

Note that you can always losslessly convert between DER and DSER without
needing the schema, and if you convert one way and then back other way it
will be the same as it was before.

This file also defines SDER (Semi-Distinguished Encoding Rules) and DSDER.
SDER is a subset of BER but a superset of DER, which is usable in case you
do not need canonical form but do not want the complexity of handling BER.
DSDER is to SDER as DSER is to DER, and removes the requirement that the
length of segments other than the last segment must be 255.

The rules for SDER are:

* Sets and key/value lists are not required to be in canonical order (but
the keys must be with the corresponding values in a key/value list). (This
also applies to a translation list.)

* TRON strings are allowed to have redundant shifts.

* ISO 2022 strings are allowed to have redundant escape codes to designate
C and G sets, and are allowed even if not immediately before the character
being encoded as a part of that set. Furthermore, a 94-set is allowed to be
designated as G0 or G1 rather than only G0.

* Default values are not required to be omitted.

* For decimal numbers, plus signs are allowed (but not mandatory, even for
the exponent), and the decimal-mark is allowed to (but not required to)
have extra digits afterward, and trailing zeros are allowed. (Scientific
numbers are still required to have exactly one digit preceding the decimal
mark, but ordinary real numbers are not subject to this restriction.)

* For binary real numbers, the significand is not required to be an odd
number, and it is not required to use the smallest number of bytes. (It
is still required to use base 2; base 8 and 16 are not allowed.)

* For Generalized time, a fractional number of seconds is allowed even if
the fractional number is zero, and trailing zeros are allowed.

* Bit strings with named bits is allowed to have trailing zeros, but the
total number of bits cannot exceed the maximum number of bits defined in
the schema, rounded up to a multiple of eight bits, unless the schema is
intended to be extensible.

* The number of bytes for encoding the length (not applicable for SDSER)
is not required to be the smallest possible number of bytes, but it is
not allowed to be more bytes than would be needed for the longest allowed
length of that item, or for the entire file if that would be smaller.
(Some of these limits may be implementation-dependent, or may depend on
the application in ways that are not specified in the schema.)

* The restrictions in DER for ISO 8601 types do not apply except that
commas are not allowed. (UTC time and Generalized time are not considered
to be ISO 8601 types.)

* Integers can be encoded in more than the minimum number of bytes, but it
is not allowed to be encoded in more bytes than is required for the longest
possible allowed value of that field (for example, a signed 32-bit integer
can be encoded as four bytes and a unsigned 32-bit integer can be encoded
as five bytes, regardless of the value; they may also be encoded in less
than that many bytes if the value fits).

